<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    `6-06`: the message-traffic driver for the webhooks-isolation load proof
    (load/reports/2026-08-23-webhooks-load-proof.md). Deliberately a black box - no ProjectReference
    into any Ago.Chat.* source, only the same public SignalR/HTTP surface a real widget or console
    would use (Microsoft.AspNetCore.SignalR.Client, the same package ago-widget/ago-console ship) - so
    the latency this measures is genuinely client-observed, not shortcut through internals.

    Deviation from this project's stated load-test tool (docs/runbooks/load-testing.md: k6): k6 is not
    installed in this sandboxed session, and installing it here would mean downloading and running an
    external binary this background session has no way to get explicit interactive confirmation for
    (see this repo's own tool-use safety rules). Separately, and just as decisive: SignalR's hub
    protocol is JSON messages framed with a 0x1e record-separator over the WebSocket, not plain
    request/response - hand-rolling that framing in a k6 script from scratch, untested, risked
    measuring bugs in this driver's own reimplementation rather than the real system. Using the real
    .NET SignalR client instead - the same library this project's own concurrency/integration tests
    already use by hand for exactly this reason (NodeDeathReconnectTests, ConnectionFanoutEndToEndTests)
    - was the honest tradeoff. Stated plainly here and in the report, not silently substituted.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.SignalR.Client" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
